  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nA bit of a breezy one out there eh? Wind gusts between 25-35 mph \nwill continue over the next few hours thanks to a strong h850 low-\nlevel jet that stems from low pressure in southern Manitoba. A cold \nfront extends down from this parent low from Grand Forks, ND down to \nthe central prairies. Ahead of the cold front, warm advective \nprecip has developed over the course of this morning. Enough \nforcing and weak instability have allowed for the production of \nfew pockets of lightning across western and central MN. For the \nrest of this afternoon/evening the highest QPF maxima is \nlocated up near central MN (i.e. ~0.1 to 0.2\"). Those totals \nfall off rather quickly the further south and west you go as dry\nair in the lowest levels is limiting development potential \nearly this afternoon. Therefore PoPs have been slightly \ndowngraded for areas across south-central and southern MN based \non continued radar trends. Once the precip ends and skies clear \nfrom west to east, lows tonight will reach near 50 across \nsouthern MN and the TC metro to the mid-40s across central MN. \n\nAfter today, there isn't much to talk about in the forecast \nother than conditions being mild and quiet. Mostly sunny skies, \nwith highs warming into the 60s to mid-70s by Tuesday. Another \nweak wave moves through Tuesday, but this time no precip is \nexpected other than perhaps another breezy day. Wednesday \nthrough Friday temps return to the mid to upper-60s while \nSaturday returns to the mid-70s (and perhaps our last summer-\nlike day of the year).",
